    "result_text": "BUILD SUCCESS \u2014 the full suite is green (the acceptance tests are the only tests in this repo).\n\n## Summary\n\nI introduced address normalization following the codebase's existing idiom.\n\n**How normalization is wired in:** `Owner.setAddress` already normalizes nothing, but its siblings do \u2014 `setTelephone` delegates to a small `TelephoneNumber` helper and `setEmail` normalizes inline. I mirrored that exactly:\n\n- **New file** `model/PostalAddress.java` \u2014 a small package-private final class (like `TelephoneNumber`) with a single `normalize` method: strip + collapse whitespace, upper-case, and expand `ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE` via a lookup map.\n- **One-line change** in `Owner.setAddress`: `this.address = PostalAddress.normalize(address);`\n\nBecause the MapStruct mapper builds the `Owner` via `setAddress`, every created owner is stored and returned normalized, and `Households` duplicate detection / shared household id automatically operate on the normalized address (both existing and candidate owners are normalized identically).\n\n**Blank rejection:** the required-field check is already the `address` DTO pattern `^.*\\S.*$`, which rejects whitespace-only input with a 400 \u2014 and since normalization only trims/uppercases/expands (it can never turn non-blank content into blank), \"blank after normalization\" is exactly \"blank before\", so the check and the normalization stay consistent. The `errorRejectsBlankAfterNormalize` test confirms the 400.\n\n**Structural-impact notes:** I touched 2 files and edited exactly one existing function (`setAddress`, CC 1, 1 changed line). I deliberately avoided editing `addOwner` in the large `OwnerRestControllerV1` (high WMC_other) or the `Households` methods \u2014 the new behaviour lives in a fresh, tiny class with near-zero surrounding complexity.",
